## Item 3: rating

Rating: 7.0 out of 10, which meets the bar of 7.0. No mockup exists, so the brief is the target. Criteria scores
(1 to 5): brief match 3.5, silhouette 3.5, proportion and appeal 3.5, shape language 3.5, value and colour 4,
materials 3.5, detail hierarchy 4, technical quality 3.5, game readiness 4, readability at 128 px 3.5, motion 3.5.
The anchor for 7.0 is the same animal with one or more clear differences. F1 is the clear difference. No cap
applies: I found no hole, no poke-through, and no floating part.

The weak points that the orchestrator named:

- Catch-light size: the sphere is 8 px on a 19 px eye, and the visible white blob is 9 x 10 px (F2).
- Wing edge contrast: low; at 128 px the 1 px sprite outline carries the wing (F4).
- Tail tuft: faint and rough (F3).
- Jaw bone without geometry: true; it affects only the fly, attack, hit, and death clips (F6).

## Findings (all nonblocking; full text in the JSON)

- F1 The wings stand out from the body; the brief asks for folded wings. Largest difference.
- F2 The catch-light is large and probably doubled in the textured build (sphere plus the kind painted glint).
- F3 The tail is faint and has a jagged edge.
- F4 The wing edge contrast is low.
- F5 The walk is small and stiff but usable; soft dark shadows under the wing and at the far leg top in some frames.
- F6 The jaw bone has no geometry.
- F7 The review card hides the head (scripts/review-cards.py:25 crops the lower half).
- F8 The cutouts are older than the last source edit; they match the final GLB silhouette at pixel level.
- F9 Two loose statements in briefs/duck.md (body list, wing wording).
- F10 The book holds an older note (line 249) that conflicts with the section 5 row; the PM rule settles it.

